<TopicPage slug="subjects/chemistry/intermediate/thermodynamics-and-enthalpy">
  <h2>Where you are, and what you will learn</h2>
  <p>
    This is an intermediate Chemistry lesson. Three foundational lessons are prerequisites:
    <strong>states of matter</strong> (heat, temperature and the particle picture behind
    every calorimetry measurement), <strong>atomic structure</strong> (what a bond is) and
    <strong>the mole and stoichiometry</strong> (every enthalpy here is quoted per mole).
    The sibling <strong>chemical bonding</strong> lesson makes the bond arithmetic concrete,
    and <strong>reaction kinetics</strong> owns the speed this page keeps delegating.
  </p>
  <p>By the end you will be able to:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>classify reactions as exothermic or endothermic from the sign of ΔH and from temperature observations;</li>
    <li>compute an approximate enthalpy change from bond energies, and explain why it is only an estimate;</li>
    <li>measure an enthalpy change by calorimetry and account for the heat that escapes;</li>
    <li>use Hess's law to reach enthalpies no single experiment can measure;</li>
    <li>apply ΔG = ΔH − TΔS to decide what is possible — and keep possibility separate from speed.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Every reaction trades energy, and two questions decide its fate: <strong>how much</strong>
    (enthalpy, ΔH) and <strong>which way does it want to go</strong> (free energy, ΔG). Burning
    releases; melting absorbs. But releases don't automatically happen — petrol sits safely in a
    tank — because a reaction must also climb an <strong>activation-energy hill</strong> before
    it can descend. This chapter installs the whole energy ledger: the two directions of heat
    flow, the bond arithmetic behind every ΔH, the calorimetry that measures them, Hess's law
    that computes the unmeasurable, and the referee (Gibbs free energy) that decides what is
    possible at all — while carefully delegating speed to the next chapter.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="two reaction paths — both may need a push uphill before settling lower (or higher) than they started" widget="plotter" config={enthalpyCfg} />
  <p>
    <strong>What to notice:</strong> follow each coloured path from left to right and read
    two separate quantities — where the path ends relative to where it started (the overall
    ΔH) and how high it must climb on the way (the activation hill). The grey jagged line
    shows that the same journey is never smooth; the endpoints, not the wobble, decide the
    sign of the ledger.
  </p>

  <p>
    The exothermic path ends below its start (ΔH negative — heat released); the endothermic path
    ends above (ΔH positive — heat absorbed, like photosynthesis). Either can proceed if the
    activation hill is climbed — by heat, or by a catalyst lowering the hill without moving the
    endpoints.
  </p>

  <h2>Exothermic and endothermic: the two directions</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Exothermic</strong> reactions transfer energy to the surroundings — the mixture's
    temperature rises, the energy profile ends lower than it started. Combustion is the
    archetype: methane burning releases ΔH = −890 kJ per mole (the kitchen hob's business
    model). Neutralisation of a strong acid with a strong alkali (ΔH ≈ −57 kJ/mol), respiration
    (the slow, enzymatically tamed combustion of glucose, ΔH = −2,803 kJ/mol), and rusting are
    all in the same family — including the humble <strong>hand warmer</strong>, which is
    iron rusting on purpose: salt water soaks through a pouch, the iron powder oxidises, and
    about 55 °C of gentle warmth results. <strong>Endothermic</strong> reactions absorb energy
    from the surroundings — the mixture cools, the profile ends higher. Photosynthesis (the
    biosphere's great endothermic engine, powered by photons) is the grand one; the
    <strong>cold pack</strong> in a first-aid kit is the domestic one: ammonium nitrate
    dissolving in water absorbs about +26 kJ per mole, dropping the pack to a few degrees
    Celsius in seconds. Thermal decompositions are the big endothermic reactions of
    industry: limestone (calcium carbonate) roasts into quicklime only above about 900 °C,
    absorbing ΔH = +178 kJ/mol — the energy bill behind every cement kiln, and a major
    slice of cement's carbon footprint. Electrolysis is endothermicity by wire: driving
    non-spontaneous reactions (splitting water, extracting aluminium) with electrical
    energy instead of heat. Note that both pack chemistries and both kiln chemistries obey
    the same diagrams — the sign of ΔH is a direction, not a judgement.
  </p>
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> "exothermic reactions need heat to start" must mean
  heat is absorbed. Starting and netting are different books: a candle needs a match
  (the activation hill), then pours out heat forever (net ΔH strongly negative). The match
  is the push; the descent is the reaction. Energy diagrams like the widget's exist precisely
  to keep these two quantities — hill height and overall drop — separate in your head.</p>
  <p>
    <strong>So far:</strong> ΔH labels the direction of heat flow — out for burning and hand
    warmers, in for cold packs and limestone kilns — and the match proves only that the hill
    needs a push, not that the reaction absorbs heat. The next question is where the energy
    actually lives, and the answer is bonds.
  </p>

  <h2>Bond arithmetic: where the energy actually lives</h2>
  <p>
    Why do some reactions release energy and others absorb it? Bonds. <strong>Breaking a
    bond always costs energy</strong>; <strong>forming one always pays it back</strong>. A
    reaction's enthalpy change is roughly the difference:
    <strong>ΔH ≈ (energy to break all bonds) − (energy released forming new ones)</strong>.
    Worked example: H₂ + Cl₂ → 2HCl. Break H–H (436 kJ/mol) and Cl–Cl (243): total 679.
    Form two H–Cl bonds (2 × 431 = 862): payback 862. ΔH = 679 − 862 = <strong>−183
    kJ/mol</strong> — exothermic, and close to the measured −185. This is also the deep
    answer to "why do fuels burn?": fuel molecules (C–H, C–C bonds, moderate strengths)
    rearrange into CO₂ and H₂O, whose bonds (C=O at 799, O–H at 463, in strong combinations)
    are collectively much stronger. The flame is the difference between average bonds held
    and better bonds made. It also explains endothermic reactions honestly: photosynthesis
    builds glucose, whose bonds are (on the whole) <em>weaker-arranged</em> than the strong
    O₂ and water bonds it dismantles — the deficit is paid in sunlight.
  </p>

  <h2>Measuring ΔH: calorimetry</h2>
  <p>
    Enthalpy is measured by watching a known mass of surroundings change temperature:
    <strong>q = m·c·ΔT</strong> (from the thermal physics chapter). Worked example: burn 0.50 g
    of ethanol under 200 g of water in a copper can; the water warms by 20 °C. Heat delivered:
    q = 0.200 × 4,186 × 20 ≈ 16,700 J. That came from 0.50 g, and ethanol is 46 g/mol, so per
    mole: 16.7 × (46/0.5) ≈ <strong>1,540 kJ/mol</strong>. The accepted value is 1,371 — the
    gap is real and instructive: heat escaped to the room, heated the can, and vaporised fuel
    before combustion finished. Simple "coffee-cup" calorimetry always under-measures; the
    fixes (insulation, flame shields, temperature extrapolation back to the moment of
    burning) are engineering honesty, and the gap between measured and true is itself a
    standard exam calculation about experimental error.
  </p>
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> "breaking bonds releases energy" — a belief that follows
  naturally from hearing "energy is stored in bonds" and inverts the truth. Bonds are a
  <em>low</em>-energy state: pulling partners apart costs energy, letting them snap together
  releases it. If breaking released energy, everything in the universe would be flying apart
  while warming up. The stored-energy picture survives in amended form: energy is stored in
  the <em>difference</em> between weak and strong arrangements — which is what the
  broken-minus-formed arithmetic computes.</p>
  <p>
    <strong>So far:</strong> bonds set the enthalpy and calorimetry measures it — always a
    little short, because heat leaks. Some enthalpies cannot be measured even in principle
    by one clean experiment, and that is where Hess's law takes over.
  </p>

  <h2>Hess's law: enthalpy is path-independent</h2>
  <p>
    Enthalpy is a <em>state function</em> — it depends on where you start and end, not the
    route. Germain Hess saw the consequence in 1840: <strong>add reaction steps like algebra
    and the enthalpies add</strong>. The classic use: carbon to CO₂ directly is hard to
    measure (carbon also makes CO), but the two-step route is easy — C + ½O₂ → CO (ΔH =
    −111 kJ/mol) and CO + ½O₂ → CO₂ (ΔH = −283). Sum: <strong>−394 kJ/mol</strong>, the
    true ΔH of combustion of carbon. Every "formation enthalpy" table in the back of every
    textbook is Hess's law industrialised: measure a library of formation reactions, then
    construct any target reaction from them like LEGO. The method survives into biochemistry
    untouched — the energy budgets of metabolism are Hess cycles with enzymes as the
    intermediate stations.
  </p>

  <h2>The referee: entropy and Gibbs free energy</h2>
  <p>
    ΔH alone cannot say whether a reaction "wants" to happen — cold packs absorb heat and
    still proceed enthusiastically. The missing ingredient is <strong>entropy</strong>, S:
    the dispersal of energy and matter, the tendency of things to spread out (ice melting,
    gas expanding, your bedroom). The referee is <strong>Gibbs free energy</strong>:
    <strong>ΔG = ΔH − TΔS</strong> — a reaction is possible when ΔG is negative, i.e. when
    heat release (negative ΔH) and/or entropy gain (positive ΔS, weighted by temperature)
    win. Ice melting above 0 °C absorbs heat but wins on entropy; dissolving ammonium
    nitrate wins the same way — the crystal's tidy order costs less than the freedom the
    dissolved ions gain. Two cautions: <strong>spontaneous does not mean fast</strong>
    (diamond → graphite is spontaneous — over geological timescales; kinetics, the next
    chapter, owns speed and the activation hill); and spontaneity can <strong>flip with
    temperature</strong>, since T multiplies the entropy term — which is exactly why
    endothermic-but-entropy-driven processes (dissolving, melting, boiling) are made
    favourable by heat. Hold the two questions apart — "how much energy?" and "will it
    happen?" — and the whole of thermodynamics becomes legible.
  </p>
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> "reactions only happen if they give out heat — endothermic
  ones must be forced." The cold pack disproves it: ammonium nitrate dissolving absorbs
  about +26 kJ per mole and proceeds enthusiastically on its own, because the referee counts
  entropy too. When the freedom the dissolved ions gain outweighs the heat absorbed, ΔG is
  negative and the reaction runs — ice melting above 0 °C strikes the same bargain with the
  surroundings.</p>

  <h2>Activation energy: why the fuel waits</h2>
  <p>
    The petrol in a tank, the flour in the bin, the oxygen all around — a thermodynamic
    bonfire waiting for a match. The barrier is <strong>activation energy (Ea)</strong>: the
    climb needed to reach the transition state where old bonds are half-broken and new ones
    half-made. Collisions must deliver enough kinetic energy, in the right orientation, to
    clear it — which is why heating helps (more molecules over the hurdle) and why sparks
    and flames work (a local hot spot seeds the exothermic descent, whose released heat then
    recruits the neighbours: the chain reaction of fire). The energy profile's hill height
    is the difference between a reaction that happens and one that doesn't, regardless of
    its ΔG — graphite "wants" to be nothing but graphite is kinetically immortal, petrol is
    thermodynamically desperate and kinetically patient. A <strong>catalyst</strong> is the
    third option: a different route with a lower hill (it holds reactants in a geometry
    that pre-pays part of the climb), moving neither endpoint. The kinetics chapter turns
    this hill into mathematics.
  </p>

  <h2>Check yourself</h2>
  <p>Try these before moving on. Feedback follows each answer.</p>
  <ol>
    <li>A hand warmer warms; a cold pack cools. Give the sign of ΔH for each and name the reaction inside each.</li>
    <li>Using bond energies, why does burning methane release energy? Answer in terms of bonds broken and formed.</li>
    <li>Petrol sits safely in a tank beside air, yet its combustion is strongly exothermic. Which two ideas explain the calm?</li>
  </ol>
  <p>
    <strong>Feedback:</strong> (1) The hand warmer is exothermic, ΔH negative — iron rusting
    on purpose; the cold pack is endothermic, ΔH positive (about +26 kJ per mole) — ammonium
    nitrate dissolving. (2) The C–H and C–C bonds broken are, on average, weaker than the
    C=O and O–H bonds formed in carbon dioxide and water — the flame is the difference
    between average bonds held and better bonds made. (3) Activation energy and kinetics:
    nothing in the tank has climbed the hill to the transition state, and spontaneous never
    means fast — petrol is thermodynamically desperate and kinetically patient until a spark
    localises enough energy.
  </p>

  <h2>Key takeaways</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Exothermic reactions release heat (ΔH negative, surroundings warm); endothermic reactions absorb it (ΔH positive, surroundings cool).</li>
    <li>Breaking bonds costs energy; forming bonds pays it back — ΔH is roughly broken minus formed.</li>
    <li>Calorimetry (q = m·c·ΔT) always under-measures a little; the gap is experimental honesty, not failure.</li>
    <li>Hess's law: enthalpy is path-independent, so reaction steps — and their enthalpies — add like algebra.</li>
    <li>ΔG = ΔH − TΔS decides possibility; entropy can pay for an endothermic reaction, and temperature can flip the verdict.</li>
    <li>Spontaneous never means fast: activation energy is the hill, and the kinetics chapter owns speed.</li>
    <li>A catalyst lowers the hill without moving either endpoint.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Where this goes next</h2>
  <p>
    The triangle of intermediate physical chemistry completes here: <strong>reaction
    kinetics</strong> turns the activation hill of this chapter into mathematics, and
    <strong>chemical equilibrium</strong> uses ΔG's verdict to say how far a reaction goes
    before it stops. Read them in either order — this page is the foundation both stand on.
  </p>

  <h2>Going deeper</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Fuel values and the arithmetic of diet and diesel.</strong> Express enthalpies per
    gram and the world's energy choices become comparisons: methane 50 kJ/g, petrol about
    44, hydrogen a stunning 120 (but a gas that must be compressed or chilled), lithium-ion
    batteries, for scale, store about 0.9 kJ/g of <em>electrical</em> energy. Food labels
    run the identical calorimetry — a bomb calorimeter burning dried food — and the
    familiar 4/9/4 kJ per gram for carbohydrate/fat/protein are just combustion enthalpies
    corrected for human metabolism. Every energy debate, from tank range to diet plates, is
    this chapter's arithmetic in public.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Standard enthalpies: the fair fight.</strong> Published ΔH values are
    <strong>standard enthalpies</strong> — measured with everything in its standard state
    (298 K, 100 kPa, solutions at 1 mol/L), so different reactions can be compared fairly.
    The symbol ΔH° carries the asterisk of fairness; quoting "the enthalpy of combustion of
    methane" without conditions is like quoting a lap time without saying which circuit.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Temperature and spontaneity: the three regimes.</strong> ΔG = ΔH − TΔS sorts
    every reaction into one of four fates. Negative ΔH, positive ΔS: always spontaneous
    (combustion). Positive ΔH, negative ΔS: never (assembling a tidy sandcastle in a
    hurricane). The two mixed cases are temperature's: endothermic-but-order-increasing
    reactions run only cold; exothermic-but-order-decreasing ones run only hot. Industrial
    chemists read this table the way engineers read load limits — it says which processes
    are even on the menu before economics votes.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>From ΔG to equilibrium.</strong> The connection this chapter reaches toward:
    ΔG° and the equilibrium constant K are the same information in two currencies
    (ΔG° = −RT·ln K). A "strongly spontaneous" reaction is simply one with an enormous K —
    which is why the equilibrium chapter's Le Chatelier predictions and this chapter's
    ΔG verdicts never disagree. The advanced tier makes the bridge explicit; for now, hold
    the slogan: <em>thermodynamics says how far, kinetics says how fast, and they are
    independent warrens</em>.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Limits of the bond-energy method.</strong> Bond enthalpies are <em>averages</em>
    across many molecules, so bond-arithmetic ΔH values are estimates — good to within
    about 10% for simple gas reactions, poor in solution, misleading with ions (hydration
    energies dominate there). Calorimetry is where the truth comes from; bond arithmetic is
    where intuition comes from. And a live frontier: entropic contributions are now
    computable well enough that drug design screens candidates by ΔG before anyone
    synthesises them — the referee of this chapter, running at industrial scale.
  </p>
